# Incremental image for the 1.600-bit weight format.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT A FULL BUILD, AND WHAT YOU NEED BEFORE IT
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# THIS FILE IS INCREMENTAL. It does not build llama.cpp; it starts from an
# image that already has a CONFIGURED BitNet tree at /app/bitnet -- that is,
# /app/bitnet/build with its CMakeCache.txt, and cmake, ninja and gcc on PATH.
# That base image is NOT in this repository and is not published anywhere. You
# have to build it yourself, from BitNet's own instructions, at the commit
# integration/README.md pins:
#
#     git clone --recursive https://github.com/microsoft/BitNet.git
#     git -C BitNet checkout 0b341e582afbf9e1011f24744b554c96a3477eb5
#     git -C BitNet submodule update --init --recursive
#     # ...then a Dockerfile of your own that installs clang>=18, runs
#     # cmake -B build, and builds the llama-cli target.
#
# Building llama.cpp from a clean clone means compiling every translation unit.
# The t5b integration adds one enum value, one traits entry, one nbytes case,
# two new source files and a dispatch branch. Recompiling ggml from scratch to
# find out whether five files link is the wrong trade -- so this image starts
# from the configured tree, drops the new sources in, runs the integration
# script, and asks ninja to rebuild only what changed.
#
# Pass your base image with --build-arg BASE=<your image>.
#
# WHAT MAKES RE-RUNNING apply_integration.py SAFE
# The base image's sources are ALREADY patched -- it was built from them. The
# script is anchor-based with a _require_once guard, so a second application
# over an already-patched file is exactly the case that guard exists for, and it
# was verified against a tree extracted from this same image before this file
# was written. If an anchor has moved the build fails loudly here rather than
# producing a binary that silently carries the old kernel.
#
# WHAT THIS IMAGE DOES NOT CHANGE
# The i2_s path. It is the control arm of every A/B this project runs, and a
# result measured against a moved baseline is not a result. The integration adds
# GGML_TYPE_T5B = 43 alongside; it does not touch ggml-cpu-i2s.c or any i2_s
# branch.
#
# llama-bench and llama-batched-bench are built alongside llama-cli.
# batched-bench is the concurrency measurement -- many sequences decoded in
# parallel, which is a different route to high arithmetic intensity than a long
# prompt is. Section 6.1 listed it as untested; it was a build target away, and
# saying "untested" without checking whether the tool was two lines from
# existing is the same mistake as reporting a blocker without probing it.
# A base image built the usual way carries only llama-cli, because that target
# is named specifically to avoid the default ALL target pulling in
# llama-ui-assets. Throughput claims deserve upstream's own measurement tool,
# with its repetitions and standard deviation, rather than a shell loop around
# llama-cli written by the party making the claim.
#
# Build, from the root of this repository:
#
#     docker build -f Dockerfile.t5b --build-arg BASE=<your base> -t bitnet-t5b .
#
# The base is an ARGUMENT, not a constant, because on 2026-09-06 the base image
# vanished from the host it was built on: `docker images` went from six bitnet-*
# tags to one, and `docker build` failed with "pull access denied" because
# BuildKit fell back to trying the registry for an image that no longer existed
# locally. Nothing in the project pruned it. An image is not a durable artefact
# and a Dockerfile that names one is not a reproduction recipe on its own.
#
# Re-running apply_integration.py over an already-patched tree is the case its
# _require_once guard exists for and was verified against.
ARG BASE=bitnet-verify-run
FROM ${BASE}

# The kernel, the glue, and the script that installs them. Copied rather than
# mounted so the image is self-contained and the build is reproducible from the
# tag alone.
# The script resolves its source directory as <its parent>/../src, so the repo
# layout has to be preserved -- a flat copy would make it look one directory up
# and fail. (Override it with --sources if you lay the image out differently.)
COPY src/ /work/src/
COPY integration/apply_integration.py /work/integration/apply_integration.py

# Install into the source tree, then rebuild. The positional argument is the
# BitNet checkout, not the llama.cpp subdirectory. --target llama-cli because
# the default ALL target pulls in llama-ui-assets, which downloads a bundle
# from HuggingFace during compilation and fails on a missing loading.html.
RUN python3 /work/integration/apply_integration.py /app/bitnet \
 && cmake --build /app/bitnet/build --config Release --target llama-cli -j 4 \
 && cmake --build /app/bitnet/build --config Release --target llama-bench -j 4 \
 && cmake --build /app/bitnet/build --config Release --target llama-batched-bench -j 4 \
 && cmake --build /app/bitnet/build --config Release --target llama-perplexity -j 4

# Fail here rather than at run time if the type never made it into the binary.
# A green build is not evidence that the kernel is present -- this project has
# shipped a "deployed" binary carrying the old code before, and only grepping
# the artefact caught it.
#
# GREP THE RIGHT ARTEFACT. The first version of this check looked in
# bin/llama-cli and failed on a build that was entirely correct: llama-cli links
# dynamically, and the type-traits table with the "t5b" type_name lives in
# libggml-base.so. A presence check aimed at the wrong file reports absence, and
# absence is exactly what it is supposed to be able to prove.
RUN set -e; \
    hit=$(grep -rlF t5b /app/bitnet/build/bin/ 2>/dev/null || true); \
    echo "carrying t5b: $hit"; \
    test -n "$hit" || (echo "FATAL: no artefact in build/bin carries the t5b type name" && exit 1); \
    nm -D /app/bitnet/build/bin/libggml-base.so 2>/dev/null | grep -i t5b || true; \
    grep -c . /dev/null || true
